Jennifer, Len, and List,

Just thought I would provide one more thought to your problem.  Conroy
et al. have a paper in press in Ecology entitled, Efficient estimation
of abundance for patchily distributed populations via two-phase,
adaptive sampling.  I am not sure when it will be out, but I have seen
the proof so it should be coming out soon.  Essentially, the paper
describes a way to estimate occupancy on all selected or random sites
(phase 1); the second phase kicks in when some threshold of detection
occurs and then a more intensive method of sampling (e.g. CMR and
distance sampling) can be used to estimate abundance.  So for your
situation as I understand it, you could use phase 1 to "map" the areas
thet are normally distributed and use distance sampling for schools.
Through a joint likelihood you get detection probabilities and
abundance.

Just a thought.

James

Len Thomas wrote:
> > Hi Jennifer,
> >
> > I didn't see any replies to your question on the list, so I'll have a
> > stab at it.  If anyone else did reply to you off the list, can you
> > please collate or summarize the responses into 1 email and post them,
> > so they are in the archive for others to see.
> >
> > If you can predict in advance where the high rockfish concentrations
> > are likely to be, you could consider stratifying the survey into high
> > density and low density areas.  The high density stratum does not have

> > to be one continuous polygon.  You could use the same survey methods
> > in this stratum, or something different.  But if the food location is
> > not predictable then this will not work.
> >
> > Again, if it were not an ephemeral resource the fish were cueing on,
> > you could consider spatial density surface modelling (Chapter 4 of the

> > Advanced Distance Sampling book) rather than the conventional
> > design-based methods for encounter rate -- but if you don't have the
> > right covariates available with will not work either.
> >
> > If the feeding aggregations are easy to spot using a "fast" survey
> > method that can quickly cover a large part of your study area, you
> > could consider using that method for the feeding aggregations, and
> > sticking to the regular line transect method for the non-feeding fish,

> > and then summing the two abundance estimates from the different
> > surveys to get the total abundance.  If you encounter a feeding
> > aggregation on the regular line transect survey you would ignore it,
> > as it is part of the "fast" survey.  But this may not work in your
> > case if it's not easy to unambiguously define what constitutes a
> > feeding aggregation, or if a "fast" survey is not possible.
> >
> > Perhaps others have further suggestion?
> >
> > Cheers,
> >
> >  - Len
> >

