Hi Jeff, Len, Lucia and all,

I will try to summarize here the problem, as I know Lucia's work. The =
main
issue is that the survey had not design to achieve equal coverage
probability, which means that to estimate abundance she needs to use the
model-based method instead of the design-based method. The process is, =
then,
(1) to obtain the detection function in Distance, (2) to obtain the
probability of detection for each observation, (3) to apply the
Horvitz-Thompson estimator to estimate the number of groups in each =
segment
of the transect line (each sampling unit), (4) to run GLMs or GAMs
(typically in R) to model abundance using the estimated number of groups =
as
response variable.

So, once Lucia has the best possible detection function, she needs to =
obtain
the probabilities of detection for each sighting. I have been doing this
myself using Distance 6 within the CODA project (we are testing the beta
version here), using the MRDS engine which includes an interface with R, =
and
then with a simple R code we can obtain the probabilities of detection =
for
each sighting. But for some reason, when doing exactly the same in =
Distance
5, using the MRDS engine, Distance gives me an error: "Error: =
Encountered a
problem while executing the data creating query 'qry17_Ddf." (being 17 =
the
identification number of the model), and it doesn't runt he MRDS. I was
exploring a bit but I couldn't find the reason for this error.=20

But I don't know if there is an easy way to extract the probabilities of
detection from the MCDS engine, as it is with MRDS and R. Before having =
MRDS
I used to extract it from the detection function equation, knowing the
values for each covariate, but it was a rather time-consuming work, so I
wonder if there is an easier way to extract that information from =
Distance.
It must be somewhere, right? As it is for MRDS?

I hope this helps in the discussion.

All the best,
Ana=20

