MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I don't know.  I'm not sure what the concern is though.  In one case,
you're modelling detectability.  In the other case, you're modelling how
density varies spatially.  In both cases, the covariates are treated as
constants.  No new issue arises if you use the same covariate in both
models.  There is an issue of propagation of error, because the
detectabilities are estimates, and you need some way to allow for this
when fitting the density surface, e.g. by bootstrapping the 2-stage
procedure.  But this is the case whether or not you have a covariate in
common between the models.

Steve Buckland

Kabelo Senyatso wrote:
> > Dear all,
> > Thanks Steve for the clarification below. I had also been worried about
> > the possible circularity in the use of a variable as a detectability
> > covariate and then to estimate density. Is this written up anywhere
> > (distance books, manuscripts etc.).
> >
> > Regards,
> > Kabelo
> >
> > ---------------------------- -------------------------------------
> >
> > Yes, this is possible.  The mcds modelling adjusts for the effect of the
> > covariate on detectability, but the resulting density estimates may well
> > be correlated with the same covariate.  There is no reason why you
> > shouldn't model that relationship.
> >
> > Steve Buckland
